sources:
    {"organization":"Atomic Heritage Foundation","title":"Klaus Fuchs","url":"https://www.atomicheritage.org/profile/klaus-fuchs"}

definition:
    German-born theoretical physicist (1911–1988), a Communist who fled the Nazis for Britain — then became the most valuable Soviet spy inside the American bomb project. At Los Alamos he worked on the implosion calculations for Fat Man; at a now-famous 1947 meeting in London he handed Moscow not only full fission-bomb designs but sketches of early hydrogen-bomb thinking. Arrested in 1950 through decrypted Soviet cables (the VENONA effort) and confessed, he served 14 years in prison, then moved to East Germany to direct a physics institute.
